1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,950
Two sections ago, 6.6 gave you a way to score one agent's action: can it be undone,

2
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,945
and what does it cost if it's wrong.

3
00:00:06,945 --> 00:00:11,600
This lecture applies the identical question to everything else an AI feature can do,

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,595
not just the actions an agent takes.

5
00:00:13,595 --> 00:00:16,145
That's the entire idea behind a risk register.

6
00:00:16,145 --> 00:00:20,467
Not a document that performs caution with checkboxes and adjectives, a list of

7
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:25,122
specific, named risks, each one scored the same honest way, each one owned by a real

8
00:00:25,122 --> 00:00:27,505
person, each one dated for its next review.

9
00:00:27,505 --> 00:00:30,886
Five categories cover almost everything you'll actually find.

10
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:33,602
The rest of this section walks each one in depth.

11
00:00:33,602 --> 00:00:36,705
This lecture gives you the register itself, and the map.

12
00:00:36,705 --> 00:00:38,866
Build it before you need it, not after.

13
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:43,522
Every one of these five categories is cheaper to catch in a design review than in an

14
00:00:43,522 --> 00:00:47,734
incident review, and a risk register's entire value is moving that discovery

15
00:00:47,734 --> 00:00:52,001
earlier, back when a mitigation is still a code change instead of a headline.

16
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:56,656
Think about the feature you're closest to shipping right now while you read the next

17
00:00:56,656 --> 00:00:56,989
slide.

18
00:00:56,989 --> 00:01:01,533
This works far better against something real than against an abstraction, the same

19
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:04,359
rule as every worksheet this course has handed you.

20
00:01:04,909 --> 00:01:09,720
Read the blast radius column the same way you read it in Section six: not a severity

21
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:14,245
label someone assigned by feel, but the answer to two specific questions, asked

22
00:01:14,245 --> 00:01:16,135
honestly, for this specific risk.

23
00:01:16,135 --> 00:01:19,915
Data and privacy scores high for a structural reason worth naming.

24
00:01:19,915 --> 00:01:24,669
Once a person's personal information has actually been seen, by a model provider, a

25
00:01:24,669 --> 00:01:29,022
log, a support agent, undoing that is not a technical problem you can patch.

26
00:01:29,022 --> 00:01:33,718
That's what makes it different from a regulatory gap, which is real, and costly if

27
00:01:33,718 --> 00:01:36,926
caught late, but genuinely fixable once you've found it.

28
00:01:36,926 --> 00:01:41,679
Prompt injection scores high for a related but distinct reason: it can act before a

29
00:01:41,679 --> 00:01:46,433
human ever reviews the output, the same category of problem 6.1's workflow test was

30
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:49,068
built to catch before anyone builds around it.

31
00:01:49,068 --> 00:01:53,535
What about bias and fairness, the row that earns its high score for nearly the

32
00:01:53,535 --> 00:01:54,451
opposite reason?

33
00:01:54,451 --> 00:01:56,456
It doesn't act fast, it hides well.

34
00:01:56,456 --> 00:02:00,923
A feature can run for months producing a quietly unequal outcome before anyone

35
00:02:00,923 --> 00:02:05,505
notices the pattern in aggregate, because no single case looks wrong on its own.

36
00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:10,676
The operational row looks like the mildest one on the table, and for most features

37
00:02:10,676 --> 00:02:14,225
it is, provided the "if someone's paged" clause actually holds.

38
00:02:14,225 --> 00:02:18,508
An operational failure with no owner and no page isn't medium risk any more.

39
00:02:18,508 --> 00:02:22,846
It's whatever the worst untreated version of that failure looks like, running

40
00:02:22,846 --> 00:02:26,509
unattended for as long as it takes someone to notice by accident.

41
00:02:26,509 --> 00:02:29,833
4471 is exactly this row, not a hypothetical version of it.

42
00:02:29,833 --> 00:02:34,510
Nobody had built a signal for "the summary silently drops the deadline," so the gap

43
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:39,073
sat there, technically operational, low drama, until a human happened to open the

44
00:02:39,073 --> 00:02:41,891
original ticket and catch what the summary missed.

45
00:02:41,891 --> 00:02:43,806
The right column isn't decoration.

46
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:48,483
It's telling you exactly where the rest of this section goes deep on each category,

47
00:02:48,483 --> 00:02:50,737
in the same order this table lists them.

48
00:02:50,737 --> 00:02:55,357
If a row here feels thin, that's the lecture to open next, not a gap to guess your

49
00:02:55,357 --> 00:02:56,033
way through.

50
00:02:56,583 --> 00:02:58,960
Notice the bad column isn't wrong, exactly.

51
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:01,391
Every sentence in it is true and reasonable.

52
00:03:01,391 --> 00:03:05,757
It's just not a risk register yet, it's the version of "important" the register

53
00:03:05,757 --> 00:03:06,751
exists to replace.

54
00:03:06,751 --> 00:03:11,283
The same move you learned for a launch approver in 6.6 applies here identically: a

55
00:03:11,283 --> 00:03:15,593
team can't be accountable for anything, because accountability that belongs to

56
00:03:15,593 --> 00:03:17,527
everyone quietly belongs to no one.

57
00:03:17,527 --> 00:03:19,793
"Legal" isn't a person who checks a date.

58
00:03:19,793 --> 00:03:24,269
A named person is, and that's the difference between a register someone maintains

59
00:03:24,269 --> 00:03:28,690
and a document that looked thorough the day it was written and stale by the next

60
00:03:28,690 --> 00:03:29,132
release.

61
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:33,664
Specificity is doing the same work here as it does everywhere else in this course.

62
00:03:33,664 --> 00:03:38,195
"We should think about prompt injection" and "an uploaded PDF's text can currently

63
00:03:38,195 --> 00:03:42,671
reach the system prompt unescaped" are not the same sentence, and only the second

64
00:03:42,671 --> 00:03:45,213
one is a row someone could actually close out.

65
00:03:45,213 --> 00:03:49,689
This is also why the register belongs to product, not to legal or security alone.

66
00:03:49,689 --> 00:03:54,000
The person who can name the actual input path, or the actual feature behaviour

67
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,587
behind a bias risk, is usually whoever wrote the feature's spec in the first place,

68
00:03:58,587 --> 00:04:03,007
not a reviewer meeting the feature for the first time in a compliance sync three

69
00:04:03,007 --> 00:04:04,113
weeks before launch.

70
00:04:04,663 --> 00:04:09,151
Say the honest caveat, because "under review" is where risk register rows go to

71
00:04:09,151 --> 00:04:09,832
quietly die.

72
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:14,491
A mitigation is a specific change: this input gets sanitised before it reaches the

73
00:04:14,491 --> 00:04:19,036
prompt, this field gets redacted before logging, this category of request gets a

74
00:04:19,036 --> 00:04:20,058
human in the loop.

75
00:04:20,058 --> 00:04:23,865
"Someone is looking into it" describes a meeting that might happen.

76
00:04:23,865 --> 00:04:28,466
It doesn't lower a single risk's actual blast radius, and a register full of rows

77
00:04:28,466 --> 00:04:33,125
like that gives a false sense of coverage to anyone who skims it without checking.

78
00:04:33,125 --> 00:04:37,726
If the honest mitigation today is "we haven't built the fix yet," write that down

79
00:04:37,726 --> 00:04:42,271
exactly, with a date it's due, rather than dressing up an open risk as a handled

80
00:04:42,271 --> 00:04:42,498
one.

81
00:04:42,498 --> 00:04:46,986
An honest, open row is worth more than a closed one that isn't actually closed.

82
00:04:46,986 --> 00:04:51,702
A stale register carries its own specific danger, close cousin to the one 7.4 named

83
00:04:51,702 --> 00:04:52,952
for a stale scorecard.

84
00:04:52,952 --> 00:04:56,871
It doesn't just fail to help, it actively reassures the wrong people.

85
00:04:56,871 --> 00:05:01,303
A launch reviewer who sees five rows marked mitigated, dated eight months ago,

86
00:05:01,303 --> 00:05:03,689
reasonably assumes the feature is covered.

87
00:05:03,689 --> 00:05:05,507
Nobody in that meeting is lying.

88
00:05:05,507 --> 00:05:09,995
The document is simply no longer describing the feature as it currently exists.

89
00:05:10,545 --> 00:05:15,156
That's the standard worth holding every row to, and the definition's order matters:

90
00:05:15,156 --> 00:05:16,933
scored, owned, mitigated, dated.

91
00:05:16,933 --> 00:05:21,044
Skip any one of those four and the row is decoration, not risk management.

92
00:05:21,044 --> 00:05:25,322
Write the one row you're already slightly worried about, specifically, today.

93
00:05:25,322 --> 00:05:29,933
Most people opening this template for the first time already know which risk they'd

94
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:34,488
write if they were honest, and have simply never had a document that asked them to

95
00:05:34,488 --> 00:05:37,155
name it plainly, with their own name next to it.

96
00:05:37,155 --> 00:05:40,821
That first row is usually the realest thing in the whole register.

97
00:05:40,821 --> 00:05:45,377
Everything after it gets easier once the habit of writing a specific, owned, dated

98
00:05:45,377 --> 00:05:46,377
row exists at all.

99
00:05:46,377 --> 00:05:49,210
Run the exercise honestly rather than exhaustively.

100
00:05:49,210 --> 00:05:53,877
One well-scored, well-owned row you'll actually revisit is worth more this week than

101
00:05:53,877 --> 00:05:58,376
five rows copied from a generic checklist, because a generic row was never scored

102
00:05:58,376 --> 00:06:02,765
against your feature's actual blast radius in the first place, and it shows the

103
00:06:02,765 --> 00:06:05,487
moment anyone asks a follow-up question about it.

104
00:06:06,037 --> 00:06:07,693
One thing before you move on.

105
00:06:07,693 --> 00:06:12,147
Download the template and write one row for real, using a risk you can already

106
00:06:12,147 --> 00:06:14,487
picture happening to your actual feature.

107
00:06:14,487 --> 00:06:19,283
Put a name on it, yours if nobody else's is more appropriate yet, and a real date on

108
00:06:19,283 --> 00:06:22,195
the calendar, not "quarterly" as a vague intention.

109
00:06:22,195 --> 00:06:26,877
A register with one honestly maintained row beats one with twenty rows nobody will

110
00:06:26,877 --> 00:06:27,562
ever reopen.

111
00:06:27,562 --> 00:06:32,244
Next lecture goes deep on the first category from today's table: prompt injection,

112
00:06:32,244 --> 00:06:36,755
the risk most product people have heard of and fewest can actually explain well

113
00:06:36,755 --> 00:06:39,781
enough to judge whether their own feature is exposed.
